description:
We conjecture that the bounds \((k-\eta)\cdot (n-1)/n\) on both the adjacency spectral radius \(\rho(G)\) and the signless Laplacian spectral radius \(q(G)\) appearing in the sufficient conditions for the existence of a spanning \(k\)-tree in a \(\frac1{k-\eta}\)-tough graph are best possible. Precisely, for every integer \(k\ge 3\) and \(\eta\in\{0,1\}\) there exist arbitrarily large orders \(n\) and connected non‑complete graphs \(G\) on \(n\) vertices such that

* \(\tau(G)=\frac1{k-\eta}\);
* \(\rho(G)=q(G)=(k-\eta)\cdot (n-1)/n\);
* \(G\) does **not** contain a spanning \(k\)-tree.

Thus the conjectured inequality cannot be improved.
